 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-prove-defers-a-process-test-it-cannot-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7 (an outside agent, 2026-10-27), found proving my.task/status-answers-before-any-turn through seon_eval and seon_prove. A deftest written at the agent's REPL that calls seon.agent-test/in-agent errors in (my.agent/prove) with \"no process store at .../target/runs/test-store-..: bin/test makes it (its plan: make-process-store!) before any shard starts; run the test through bin/test\". (my.agent/tests-reaching 'seon.test-store/shared-root!) lists the new test as reaching in-agent, which is in :seon.config.source/process-vars, yet (:deferred (my.agent/prove)) stayed [] and :at-judge 16: the test ran in the agent's fork instead of being deferred to the merge gate as my.agent/deferred says it is. Find why a test defined at the REPL is not classified a process test (the branch's reach graph not refreshed for a new test? deferral not applied to a registered proof?) and fix it at that producer. Proof: a process test defined on a branch is :deferred by (my.agent/prove), not run and not an error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-process-test-defined-on-a-branch-is-deferred-not-run}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-that-calls-the-turn-path-does-not-recurse-into-prove"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, form: in an agent, a deftest that clears (swap! seon.agent/state update :turn dissoc :observed) and calls (my.task/status), where status measures through seon.turn/observe, makes (my.agent/prove) answer :error \"test process died: {:exit 0} while #'seon.task-test/status-answers-before-any-turn ran\". Suspected cause (not confirmed): observe asks the test service (seon.agent/prove!) from inside the test service's own fork, which waits on its own run or exits. Confirm with the fork's stderr, then make a test that reaches the turn path either run its observe without asking the service for the run it is already in, or answer the typed error, never a dead child. Proof: a test that calls seon.turn/observe inside a prove run ends with its own result."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-test-in-a-prove-run-that-reaches-observe-ends-with-its-result}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-flaky-test-is-a-task"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"we shouldn't write tests that drift like that -- so a flaky test is a mining and task to fix it dispatched to an agent\". What exists: seon.proofs/flaky marks a test the runner saw green and red at one digest (:flaky true, judged red until its digest moves), and bin/prove prints \"N flaky\"; nothing more happens, so a flaky test stays red and unowned (2026-10-30: three load-sensitive agent tests held flaky by run 29's gate). Correct: the first time a test is judged flaky at a digest, root writes a task for it from a template, as seon.template/stall writes a stall's (one task per test and digest, its name; its cause pre-filled: the test, both results with their failures, the run logs' paths, the load the runs saw), released for a Seon agent to find the cause and fix it at its cause (a clock, a race, live state: never a retry or a wider bound). It is the first kind of one mechanism, a finding becomes a task (docs/health.md#a-form-too-long: a template per kind), so the shrink kinds reuse it. Deletes nothing; replaces bin/prove's print-only flaky count as the place a flaky test goes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-test-green-and-red-at-one-digest-becomes-one-task}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-ticks-are-the-same-alone-and-in-a-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-10 (Context's baseline, see seon.agent/a-done-counts-only-the-reds-its-change-reaches): seon.program-test/a-test-that-starts-a-process-reaches-the-program-it-runs and seon.render-test/a-render-reaches-no-process went over their 4B-tick budget (:seon.config.agent/test-ticks) inside a big prove and were green alone. Ticks are work done and should be the same for one test on any machine, at any load, in any company; a count that differs by what ran before is a defect of the count or of the test. The class: a budget that a process's history can turn red (AGENTS.md section 5: no assertion the machine's load can turn red; history is the same class). Candidates, to reproduce in this order (inferred, not yet run): (a) collections: garbage earlier tests left is collected inside this test's span, and the budget in seon.proofs/run-tests! counts it (seon.cost/measure takes off a tick allowance a collection; the budget may not); (b) derived data a process holds (a kept reach graph, my.test's world delay, a program db) warm in one process and built in the other, both tests walking the whole program's reach; (c) Jolt's one-way switch to threaded lazy seqs after a process's first OS thread (seon.cost's `threaded`). Reproduce: run each test alone and after the tests its shard ran before it in Context's run (the shard file names them), compare ticks and the collections in its span. Fix at the cause: a count with no collection work, or a test that makes the data it reads. Proof, red first: one of the two tests run after a test that leaves a large heap, its ticks within a fixed slack of its ticks alone."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/seon-program-test-test-starts-process-reaches-program}}
{:seon.task/name "a-check-steps-digest-takes-the-seed-of-the-store-it-runs-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 in jolt/erase-window. jolt/cite-ref at 958ffb11cd moved its standing targets (the sections and var every agent and template cites) out of seon.db/seed-tx, and its bin/prove reused the green verdicts of e2e-conflict, e2e-semantic, e2e-hairy, e2e-newns, e2e-sibling, e2e-held and swarm. Their digests (seon.proofs/step-digests: the reach of each step's functions) did not move, because a step reads the check store, which another step (seed) made from seon.db/seed-tx and seon.test-store/standing-targets-tx, and neither is in the step's reach. In the erase window the same steps were red: each merge task's create was refused, :seon.db/no-target [:var/name my.branch/merge-main!], since the check store lacked the var the merge template's instructions cite. Reproduction: on a tree whose seed lacks the template's cited var, a check step that writes a merge task keeps its old green digest and reuses it, while bin/check --steps runs it red. The check store is keyed by its seed's digest since 2026-11-09 (seon.check/seed: the link target-dir check-store-<digest of [schema-digest, seed-tx, standing-targets-tx]>). Correct: each check step that runs on the check store takes that seed digest into its digest (step-digests' input beside :env), so a changed seed moves every such step's digest and runs it. Proof, red first: a change to standing-targets-tx moves an e2e step's digest."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/change-seon-test-store-standing-targets-tx-seed}}
 {:seon.task/name "tests-leave-no-scratch-behind"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by the slow-test helpers: tests leave scratch directories they make (target/tmp/prove-* from seon.proofs-test's runs, 25 in one worktree; target/stalls-test-*; a temp dir of seon.fx-test's source-read test). Correct: each removes what it makes in a finally, and the full run's end names any target/tmp entry younger than it (a leak is red, as a thread left running is). Proof: the full run's leak check red on a test that leaves one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-scratch-stores-proof-has-a-mutation"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: seon.proofs-test/a-process-plans-on-more-scratch-stores-than-its-undo-entries plans on 11 scratch stores in turn, closing each (close-stores!). The limit it was written against is gone: with lmdb 989f3e07 LMDB makes no semaphore (each lock is a mutex of the process and a record lock on the store's lock file, docs/failure-points.md#lmdb-locks), so no count of open stores meets a system-wide table, and `lmdb-lock`'s case of 50 stores in one process proves that at the fork. Inferred by reading 2026-11-11, not run: no mutation turns it red now (without close-stores! the eleven stores stay open in the process and nothing refuses a twelfth open), so it is a check that cannot fail. Correct: delete the deftest (its claim is the fork's, proved by bin/check lmdb-lock), or restate it as the claim that a test's own store ends with the test (no env of the scratch path left in the process after the test: konserve.jolt.lmdb's envs hold none), red when close-stores! is dropped. Owner: Tests. Proof: none for a deletion; for the restatement, the test red without close-stores!."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-whose-tree-changed-under-it-is-no-verdict"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Optimization (2026-11-11): a run of onetree's seon.system-test was read as green after a tracked file was edited while it ran, and was discarded by hand. What exists: a `bin/test` shard compares the checkout's tree (```seon.fx/tree-id```) after its run with its plan's, and when they differ takes back the greens it wrote (```seon.fx/forget-greens!```), stores nothing and prints \"the tree changed since the plan: no result stored\" (test-runner/shard!), but the run's exit and its TEST OK stay as reported, so a session reads a green that judged no tree. Correct: a shard whose tree changed makes the run's last line INVALID (the tree it planned and the tree at its end) and its exit non-zero, as a died shard does; `bin/prove` and bin/check's runner read the same exit, so no caller reads it as green. Proof: a run whose test edits a tracked file of its checkout's tree ends INVALID with a non-zero exit, and stores nothing. docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache states it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-run-whose-tree-changed-under-it-is-no-verdict}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-tests-that-read-a-value-are-the-tests-reaching-its-maker"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Web (2026-11-11): jolt/defectline changed the shape of a component's error and went red in a batch on seon.ctx-test/an-agent-adds-a-datum-and-its-render, a test Web had not run; Web asked for `bin/test --reach SHAPE`, grepping the tests that read a string or shape. What exists: a value's readers are the tests whose reach includes the function that makes it, which the reach graph answers (```seon.program/tests-reaching```, an agent's `(my.agent/tests-reaching 'f)`), and `bin/prove` selects exactly them by digest; a grep of a shape finds text, not readers, and misses a test that reads it through a render. What is missing is the session's way to ask: `bin/test` names namespaces and tests, not a var's readers. Correct: `bin/test --reaching NS/VAR ...` runs the tests ```seon.program/tests-reaching``` answers for each var, over the plan's graph (test-runner's reach-graph-of), so a session changing a function runs its readers by name while it iterates; a string no var makes is no input, so it has no flag. Proof: on a small program, --reaching a function runs the test that reads its value through another function and not one that only names it in a string. docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches names it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/bin-test-reaching-runs-the-readers-of-a-var}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-red-with-no-main-verdict-is-proved-on-main-first"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-12 (Context, pairlow's fresh store, agent accf834b10555f2e): its proof green at e31, its status refused done on three :stray-red tests, two of them not its change's (a ClassCastException in seon.render-cost-test's child, a seon.source-test red). :also-red-on-main was absent because the store held no main verdict at those tests' base digests, and the agent spent its last 8 evals on them. What exists: seon.proofs/main-red calls a red main's when main's verdict at the test's base digest is red. The judge feeds it from seon.agent's done-input (stored-results), and the gate from seon.fx/run-tests (jolt/redsat, through seon.fx/reds-at). Both treat a digest with no verdict as not red, so the red falls to the task, which is unknown taken as a default. Correct, one mechanism for both readers: the reds whose base digest holds no verdict are asked of the test service once, as one run of main at the task's base commit named by exactly those tests (seon.prove's run of main). Then main-red reads the answer. Every later asker finds those verdicts in the cache at their digests, so they run at most once whoever asks. Deletes: none; the two readers move to reds-at, and agent.clj's stored-results call goes. Cost: one prove of k tests, k = the reds with no main verdict, typically 0 on a store that has been proved. Proofs, red first: a branch red on a test whose base digest has no verdict, and main red there, is told main's after one run of main; with main green there, it is the task's; a second asker runs nothing. See docs/tasks.md#the-done-rule."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-red-with-no-main-verdict-is-proved-on-main-first}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-tests-reads-hold-what-its-agent-ran"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests. Since 2026-11-12 a test that reaches a process of its own is reused at its digest only (seon.proofs/reads-reusable), because its recorded reads hold what its own process called, never what the agent or root it drives ran (context's finding: a change to seon.repl/edit! left seon.repl-test/a-namespace-edit-names-the-var-that-holds-the-text at its recorded reads). So 197 process tests rerun whenever their digest moves, the costliest keys. Plan: an eval's answer carries the names of every contracted function its eval called (seon.profile/reads over the eval's epoch, names only), not only its top form-top-fns with costs (seon.repl); seon.test-store/ask folds each answer's names into the asking test's reads (the runner's record, as profile/with-reads does for its own process); then reads-reusable takes process tests back. Estimate: names only, ~100 symbols an eval, a few KB an answer; no change to an eval's work. Proof, red first: a process test whose agent eval calls a var no reach of the test shows is not reused when that var changes. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.program/a-tests-digest-reads-what-it-ran (Seen twice on 2026-11-08: a process test's verdict misses the code run in the processes it boots or drives by text (ts/start-root!, ./seon CMD, code sent to root as a string): seon.system/start-agent changed under the root test of an agent's name across a restart (Context; now seon.system-test/a-second-boot-resumes-the-same-main) and seon.check/boot-twice under seon.system-test/a-second-boot-in-on)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/test-process-ran-code-through-string-sent-root seon.agent-test/a-process-tests-green-reads-what-its-agent-ran}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-verdict-is-an-entity-that-names-its-environment"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan Stage 1.5; owner 2026-11-14 \"pull first\": store a result shaped for how it is read back. Where seon.proofs writes a file under target/results (709 MB, 115k files), it writes a :seon.proof entity: test, status written once, everything the run read (datoms, config entries, files, env vars, pin as outside reads), the environment {:branch :commit :t} and process it ran in, its cost. stored-results reads the store in the same landing; reuse, why-red and flaky become queries. The file tree is removed after an audit to the owner. Schema agreed with data first. Owners: tests, data. Measured 2026-11-14 (tests): the file cache it replaces holds 130,784 verdict files (478 MB) and 2,051 reads.edn (13 MB) in target/results; its .size ledger keeps it under :seon.config.proofs/results-max-bytes (516.7 MB of 512 MiB), but the disk pays per file (814 MB by du, ~1.7x): entities delete that cost with the files. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.proofs/test-results-are-a-few-facts (Not ready: the owner (Sean) must say whether bin/test and bin/prove write facts through root at all: decisions.md (2026-10-28, \"Agent runs only\") and docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache keep the file cache as what reuse reads and bin/land/full-run rely on a file surviving a store erase, which contradicts the plan's \"delete the file cache\"; the built part (agent run facts) stands). Design approved by the owner 2026-11-15 (notes tests-4-design-one-queue-2026-11-15.md, reviewed by runtime-2 and flow): this is landing 12. In order: (1) FIRST, seon.prove's :main consumer takes every commit notice (today a sliding buffer of 1 drops notices [M, runtime-2]; a lossy buffer is only for a stream the newest subsumes, flow's rule): unbounded by design, or diff from the last commit seen. (2) One :seon.proof entity per (test, reads digest) on the standing store's results branch (:seon.config.proofs/results-branch), written through root's writer only, stamped :seon.tx/branch and :seon.tx/asker, one commit per run (a commit costs ~440k ticks [M, root]): test, status (write-once: a second status at one id is refused, and the refusal files a task in the test's namespace: the FLAKY ruling), basis, cost {:ticks :bytes}, failures, a cost proof's rows, and :seon.reads/key-set (re-apply 205e329bb8 as its first writer). (3) In-process tests record their key set through seon.ratom/reads-of; a commit's datom keys and the reverse seek (seon.db/results-moved, renamed seon.db/readers with 205e) MARK the verdicts they touch stale (O(d)); a marked verdict runs only when asked (a land, a prove, seon_done), eagerly only for main's head (the owner: \"Mark; eager on main\"); the daily full run goes. (4) stored-results reads the entity; then the files, reads.edn, .recent, the proved marker and :seon.test/* go. Estimate [H]: the writer and reader ~150 lines; a run's facts ~6 datoms a test x R tests in one commit (~440k + R x ~5k ticks); a commit's stale-mark one AVET seek a moved key; net src negative after the deletes. Needs: seon.prove/one-digest-whichever-runner-plans-it for dedup; data reviews the schema. File reads become store reads (tests-4, 2026-11-15): 12a (f4e4404ce3) keys a green that read the checkout raw by the files it opened ([:file p], git blob ids, Jolt's io-file-read-sink), an interim on files. In this landing a test reads the wiki and task files from the store (seon.doc's entities, as root's own context does), its run's datom reads are recorded (seon.ratom/reads-of, :seon.reads/key-set), and an edit of a page is datoms that reach exactly their readers (seon.db/readers); [:file p] and seon.profile/opened are deleted. One read record a verdict: today three (seon.profile/reads for functions, seon.profile/opened for files, seon.ratom/reads-of for datoms) become one, under one reverse lookup. Measured motive [M]: a one-line docs/decisions.md edit moved 88 tests, 78 of them only because they read one file raw; ctx.wire's 30 read only docs/prompt.md. DECIDED 2026-11-15 (tests-4 lead; reviewed by data-2, flow, deps; notes tests-4-landing-12-plan-draft-2026-11-15.md). EXTENDS seon.fx/write-verdicts! and its readers stored-results, held-reads, details, history onto the existing :seon.proof schema (0 entities on main; seon.db/results-moved exists). (1) Identity: a content id over [test reach-digest] (:seon.db/content-id, as :seon.system/id); a lookup computes it and seeks AVET once: 3.3k ticks / 5 KB via d/datoms, never a pull (14.3k / 63 KB) [M deps result/d51edc87]. (2) One read record: function reads as their form datoms, config as its entity's datoms, files as :file/text; seon.db/results-moved alone decides reuse; seon.proofs/reads-key's map and 12a's [:file p] deleted in the same landing; proof red first: a test whose only read is a config key that moves. (3) Writes: one per batch from the prove proc ({:op :tx :branch results} on :to-writer, pended), never from the batch agent (agent.clj:1815's write-verdicts! deleted). (4) A read-key set is its own entity by content digest (:seon.reads/set) that verdicts reference: ~6 datoms a rerun, not ~35k; its own cost proof. COST: a plan holds the results branch's value once (~200k ticks [M deps result/b6c9295f]) and seeks only the a tests whose digest differs from the base's (base statuses kept per base point): ~graph update (12-22M) + 0.2M + a x 3.3k, against 221M / 848 MB today [M result/d6c4f272: read-verdict 1,889 parses 157M/518 MB, held-reads ~140M/460 MB]. Deletes: the target/results file tree (after an audit to the owner), read-verdict's parse, reads.edn, reads-key's map, [:file p]. DATA-2'S REVIEW of slice 1 (2026-11-15, seon.facts.edn:222-243): (1) :seon.proof/reach-digest is its own attribute (:seon.lib/digest) and :seon.proof/id the content id over [test reach-digest]; reads-digest keeps its meaning, the content digest of what was read; a lookup is one AVET seek, ~24.5 us [M data result/dbb85658]. (2) :seon.reads/keys holds real :seon.reads/key tuples: function reads as their forms' datom keys ([:e form]), config as its entity's, files as :file/text's, the one :seon.reads/set now (slices 1 and 2 in one; no interim shape stored). (3) :route :live (and :seeded for the one-time carry-over from the file cache, which root allowed: main's current digests only, reds included, onto the results branch); :basis a :seon.db/reference {:seon.db/branch :seon.db/commit :seon.db/t}. RULING (root, 2026-11-15, replacing the earlier (a)): landing 12 moves the service's verdicts (written per batch by the prove proc) and seon.agent/test-plan's reads to the store; bin/test's shards keep the file cache (seon.fx/write-verdicts!'s file path, read by test-runner) as the ONE named transitional second way, deleted by E when bin/test becomes a thin client of the service. Why: bin/test is frozen (AGENTS.md §0), and a :verdicts op for remote clients would exist only to be deleted (no existing op takes a remote data write [M: seon.server/ops]). COST of the interim: until E, a bin/test green is not reused by the store's test-plan (and a service green not by bin/test); E's Deletes name the file cache, write-verdicts!'s file path, read-verdict, held-reads' reads.edn, evict! and history-add!. SLICE 2's interface, agreed with flow (2026-11-15 13:40): bin/test's verdicts go through the existing :prove op (seon_prove), not new ops (every op is an MCP tool; two new ops grow every session's tool list, two args on :prove do not). WRITE: :prove {:verdicts [{:test :digest :status :reads ...}]} sends verdicts as data, never :tx-data (raw tx-data from a client is a generic branch write, a second writer of the verdict shape); the prove proc builds the tx (seon.proofs/verdict-tx) and writes it by the same per-batch write as its own batches (prove.clj verdicts-write): one owner of the shape, nothing waits in its step. READ: :prove {:reuse cand} is answered by the prove proc from the results-branch value it holds (it writes them, so its reads are con